      "message": "Say what one owner may take up here\n\nA forge shared with other people has to be able to say no. Every owner,\na person or an organisation, now has a quota: repositories, agents,\nopen tasks across their repositories, and disk those repositories take.\nA refusal names what they have and what the limit is, because \"you\ncannot\" alone leaves the reader guessing whether to delete something or\nask for more; over the API it is 409 with kind over_quota. The forge's\ndefaults are 50, 25, 200 and 5 GiB, set for the whole forge with\n--quota-repos and its three companions, and for one owner with\ncairn admin quota or the quota endpoint. An owner sees theirs on their\nown page and nobody else does. This is the shape a bigger plan would\ntake: a bigger quota and nothing else.\n\nDisk is measured rather than tracked, because git changes the answer by\npacking objects with nothing happening here to record. Each repository\nis measured after a push and after a landing, and a forge that has\nnever measured looks once at startup so an upgrade does not report\neverybody at zero. Since what a push costs is not knowable until it is\nunpacked, an owner already over is refused the next one rather than the\none that crossed the line.\n\nOne thing this fixes was live and breaking pushes. The old-address\nredirect added in the previous change answered git's credential\nchallenge with a move, so every push - which always begins with that\nchallenge - followed the redirect somewhere else and died. An address\nthat names a repository which exists now is never an old address,\nwhatever the handler answered, and a name freed by a rename can be\ntaken again and is then live rather than old. A test pushes to a\nretaken name. This change had to be pushed with credentials up front to\nget past the bug it repairs.\n\nTwo projections, tags and former names, were never in the list a schema\nchange rebuilds and fsck compares. Nothing looked wrong until a\nrepository with a tag was renamed twice: the next rebuild replayed the\nlog into rows that were still there and stopped at a unique constraint,\nwhich is a forge that will not start. Both are in the list now, and a\ntest derives it from the schema so the two cannot drift again. This is\nthe rehearsal on a copy of production earning its place: no test here\nhad two renames and a tag in one log.\n\nAn agent now belongs to somebody, and that is what makes the count\npossible: it is the person who registered it, or an organisation they\nbelong to. So anybody who can sign in makes their own agents, mints\ntheir tokens, grants them capabilities on repositories they hold, and\nretires them; registering a person or an organisation stays the\noperator's, because a name in this namespace is not one owner's to hand\nout. The Agents page is everyone's now, showing what the viewer holds.\nAn agent cannot own anything, including itself, which closes a gap in\nthe owner check that only ever mattered once agents could register.\n\nChange-Id: Iquotas0099\nSigned-off-by: Mandip Adhikari <92096340+mandipadk@users.noreply.github.com>",
